stories.body:
OpenAI's security researchers built an autonomous pipeline that finds security relevant code, scans it, reproduces bugs in isolated environments, tests exploitability and drafts patches. In one run with an upcoming cyber model it reproduced 1,724 Linux kernel vulnerabilities and produced working exploits for 948 distinct local privilege escalation bugs on a stock desktop setup.

@OpenAI said the run cost $1,213,430 in API credits, about $1,279 per bug, against a hypothetical bounty value of $18.9M to $66.3M. The agents also used at least 10 exploitation techniques OpenAI calls novel. Humans review results, and OpenAI is working with Linux maintainers and Patch the Planet partners on fixes.

Why it matters: offense and defense at machine scale just got a price tag. Watch how the upcoming cyber model is gated and how fast the kernel patches land.

Intology says Locus, its automated research system, autonomously develops scientific AI models that outperform state of the art expert solutions in molecular design, using proprietary data to do it. Intology team member @HJCH0 says the lab went from state of the art autonomous post training to frontier performance on autonomous molecular design, such as antibody design, in a couple of months, and credits domain agnostic auto research improvements. @omarsar0 called it a highly recommended read, noting it covers molecular property prediction and potency prediction. If an AI research agent can beat expert built models on drug discovery tasks like potency prediction and antibody design, autonomous AI R&D starts to look like a practical tool for biotech rather than a demo.

@intology posted: "Locus sets the frontier for automating molecular design Our automated research system Locus develops scientific AI models that outperform SOTA expert solutions Unlocking the potential of scientific AI requires leveraging proprietary data. Locus achieves this autonomously"

@omarsar0 added: "Highly recommended read. Very few companies I know have achieved this leap in scaling autonomous AI R&D in science. Intology claims their Locus system sets the frontier for automating molecular design. That covers molecular property prediction, potency prediction with" @intology added: "AI R&D problems in scientific settings are often heavily data constrained. For molecular property prediction and small molecule potency prediction, Locus overcomes these problems’ limited data initialization to train models that generalize better. Locus designs solutions that"

Sarvam says Prism is a System One model for fast, accurate decisions across enterprise workflows. It scores 71.8 with a median response time of 44 milliseconds, which Sarvam calls the highest average among decision models answering in under 100 ms, and it advances the accuracy speed frontier across three decision indices among 25 models tested. Sarvam says Prism gave the same answer every time answer options were shuffled across 2,000 questions, made fewer errors than its confidence predicted in English and Indian languages, and ships with open weights so enterprises can fine tune and self host it. A sub 100ms open weights decision model aimed at guardrails, routing and trace evaluation gives enterprises a cheap, self hostable judge layer, and it is another sign of India's Sarvam competing on frontier adjacent models.

@SarvamAI posted: "Today we’re releasing Sarvam Prism, a System One model built on Sarvam 105B for fast, accurate decisions across enterprise workflows. It scores 71.8 with a median response time of 44 milliseconds, the highest average score among decision models that respond in under 100"

@SarvamAI added: "Every enterprise decides differently. We’re releasing Prism with open weights so enterprises can fine tune it on their own outcomes using simple training recipes and host it efficiently within their own infrastructure. We also support training and deployment on Sarvam’s" @VinayakGavariya added: "We just launched Sarvam Prism, and I built a Snake game to show why decision models matter. Two snakes. Same words. One eats poison. The other knows when to ask for help. Here's Decision Model vs an LLM"

Claude users can now auto connect plugins when they sign in, as long as the plugin's MCP server accepts Claude as a trusted issuer. Admins can also use enterprise managed auth, built on an open MCP extension, to connect plugins for a whole organization through their identity provider. Anthropic says the trusted issuer option is in beta on Team and Enterprise plans with partners including Atlassian, Datadog, Figma, HubSpot, Linear, Notion and Supabase. Re authorizing every tool is one of the biggest friction points for agent workflows; login time auth for MCP plugins makes company wide Claude rollouts much easier and pressures other assistants to match it.

@ClaudeDevs posted: "Users can now auto connect plugins when they log in to Claude, with MCP servers that accept Claude as a trusted issuer. If you’re building plugins for Claude, add support so users get started faster and stay connected without re authorizing."

@ClaudeDevs added: "If you’re building plugins for Claude, you can add support today:" @ClaudeDevs added: "Auto connect with Claude as the trusted issuer is in beta on Team and Enterprise plans. Available with Amplitude, Atlassian, Datadog, Dropbox, Figma, Granola, HubSpot, Linear, Mixpanel, Notion, PagerDuty, Semrush, and Supabase."

Soniox says STT v6 is the first speech recognition model that takes the voice agent's own text as input, giving it context from both sides of the conversation, and cancels the agent's playback echo while still listening to the user, even when they interrupt. Soniox says that means fewer false interruptions and clarification loops, plus significantly higher accuracy across 60+ languages for voice typing, meeting transcription, multilingual recognition and real time translation, with stronger diarization, language ID and endpoint detection in one unified model. It is available now for real time streaming and async transcription (soniox.com/blog/soniox stt v6). False barge ins and echo are among the most common failure modes in production voice agents, so building agent aware recognition into the speech model itself could make voice agents noticeably more reliable without extra pipeline work.

@soniox_ai posted: "Introducing Soniox STT v6, the world’s first speech recognition model that knows what your voice agent is saying and automatically filters out the agent’s own voice. Two connected innovations make this possible: → Agent text input gives recognition context from both sides of"

@soniox_ai added: "Speech recognition has been missing half the conversation. Soniox STT v6 is the world’s first STT model that knows what your voice agent is saying. Introducing agent text input. Developers can stream the agent’s outgoing text into STT as it is sent to TTS, giving recognition" @ashen_one added: "if you're building a voice agent that keeps cutting itself off mid sentence, soniox v6 goes after that exact problem v6 lets you stream the text your agent is about to say into the speech recognition so it knows what's coming out of the speaker, filters that out, and keeps"

Anthropic shipped Claude app upgrades: generated work opens beside the chat on iPad/iPhone, voice mode works in Apple CarPlay, and Claude on Android now behaves like a laptop app on Chromebooks and foldables. Puts Claude into the car and makes mobile a real work surface, sharpening the assistant race with ChatGPT and Gemini.

@claudeai posted: "A few upgrades to the Claude mobile apps this week: On iPad and iPhone Duo: what Claude makes now opens beside the chat instead of over it, and goes full screen when you want a closer look."

@sakhil_ai added: "A 19 year old Japanese student built a trading bot using Claude Code in just 2 days. He even used his iPad as a second screen. First night profit: $6,732. Starting capital: Just $68. Total profit so far: $750,000. Here's how his bot works The bot checks more than 50" @ArifAIHQ added: "RT @The_techAI: 19 year old Japanese student built a trading bot with Claude Code in 2 days. Used his iPad as a second monitor. First nig…"

Anthropic's Max 5x plan becomes Max ($100/month) and Max 20x becomes Ultra ($200/month); verified Ultra accounts lose the 5 hour usage window starting today. Users also report API credits bundled with the plans. Heavy Claude Code users were the most vocal about rate limits; removing the window on the top tier is a direct move in the coding agent pricing war with OpenAI.

@claudeai posted: "We’re renaming our Max plans: Max 5x → Max Max 20x → Ultra Max is still $100/month, and Ultra is still $200. If you’re a subscriber, there’s nothing you need to do. We’re also removing the 5 hour limit for verified Ultra accounts, starting today with most existing"

@StatsWire added: "Claude Opus 4.6 just DESTROYED my Antigravity usage limits!!! A few hours ago, I still had 81% of my weekly Claude/GPT quota left. Now??? • Weekly limit: 45% left • 5 hour limit: 0% left • Reset in 1 hour 11 minutes Anyone else burning through Claude usage like this on" @Heaney555 added: "@mveehkim @bensprks What? You just... open Gemini. The app on your phone, or What's the confusion? The caveat is that you need a paid (Pro or Ultra) account to see it, but that's the case for OpenAI and Anthropic's flagship models too."

OpenAI's developer team says Pando handles more pushes and fetches without changing how its engineers and agents use Git. It cut median push latency by nearly 80%, now serves nearly a petabyte of Git data a day, updates different branches in parallel, and serves fetches from reusable stored packfiles. OpenAI also contributed fixes back to Git, including cleanup after interrupted packfile downloads. Agent driven coding is multiplying Git traffic inside big labs; OpenAI rebuilding its Git backend shows source control is becoming an infrastructure bottleneck for agent heavy engineering.

@OpenAIDevs posted: "We built Pando, a new Git backend for our monorepo, to handle more pushes and fetches without changing how our engineers and agents use Git. Pando cut push latency by ~80% and now serves nearly a petabyte of Git data a day. We can grow Git capacity alongside agent usage."

@dadkins_ added: "super cool example of rearchitecting a system for the massively horizontal workloads of agents! narrows consistency to per branch, and turns the server into a pack index+cache at the cost of redundant pack transfer" @udayruddarraju added: "One of the best things we've done here at OpenAI this year. The growth of our monorepo was unprecedented and so was our approach to scaling it, while keeping compatibility with git. Huge kudos to @tnm and team!"

Deep Principle introduced Hydrogen 1.0, its first general purpose large model, aimed at helping researchers reason through scientific questions, run calculations and simulations, and turn ideas into experiments. Another entrant in the AI for science race, where labs are pitching models as research partners rather than chatbots.

@DeepPrinciple posted: "Introducing Hydrogen 1.0, Deep Principle’s first general purpose large AI model, built to support scientific discovery. Hydrogen 1.0 helps researchers reason through complex scientific questions, carry out calculations and simulations, turn research ideas into experimental"

@RussellQuantum added: "Deep Principle released Hydrogen 1.0 yesterday, an open weight model for scientific research, post trained from GLM 5.3 at 753 billion parameters, weights on Hugging Face. The headline claim is that it beats GPT 6 Astra on FrontierScience Research" @ChengleiSi added: "RT @chenru_duan: We’re introducing Hydrogen 1.0, our first open weight model for scientific research. We post trained GLM 5.3 (@Zai_org) on…"

The Allen Institute for AI (Ai2) introduced StateBench, a new evaluation of whether LLMs can track state changes over long texts, such as an object changing hands or a variable being reassigned. Ai2 says frontier models including GPT 6 Sol and Claude Opus 5.5 fell to 13% accuracy or lower on the hardest, longest problems. Long context agents and coding tools depend on keeping track of what changed and when; a public benchmark showing top models collapsing on that task gives builders a concrete weakness to test against.

@allen_ai posted: "Can LLMs track state changes over long texts like an object changing hands or a variable reassignment? On our new StateBench eval, frontier models including GPT 6 Sol & Claude Opus 5.5 fell to 13% accuracy or lower on the hardest, longest problems."

@lambdaviking added: "Fun project led by @jowenpetty ! StateBench is benchmark for long context state tracking abilities that we're already using internally to develop new Olmo models" @allen_ai added: "We tested open pretrained models + frontier models incl. Gemini 3.8 Flash, Claude Opus 5.5, & GPT 6 Sol + Luna. Frontier models did well on simpler problems. But as tasks got harder & inputs longer, accuracy dropped sharply to 13% or lower on the longest general finite problems."

Mistral AI published open weights for Mistral Large 4 ("Le Chonk"), reported as a ~1T parameter mixture of experts with 49B active. Artificial Analysis ranks it the top open weights model built outside China, and vLLM with NovaSky's SkyRL shipped day 0 RL support on the NVFP4 checkpoint. A frontier scale open model from Europe resets the open weights field outside China and gives builders a new base to fine tune.

@vllm_project posted: "Day 0 RL for @MistralAI’s Mistral Large 4 with @NovaSkyAI’s SkyRL and @vllm_project! On 8 @nvidia GB200 nodes, LoRA RL on the NVFP4 checkpoint brings AIME 2024 avg@8 from ~64% to ~75% in 75 training steps. vLLM generates rollouts, while FSDP2 or Megatron handles training."

@dextune added: "Axolotl Fine tune large MoE models with YAML configs, then export straight to GGUF Axolotl is a free, open source fine tuning framework. October added Mistral Large 4 with merge aware NVFP4 LoRA and expert parallel training, and Reflection's Beam with QLoRA configs. CPU staged" @RobertTLange added: "Mistral's Large 4 at a glance 1T param MoE (52B active), multimodal, 1M context:, 72 layers MLA + DeepSeek style sparse attention: indexer picks 2,048 keys/query, Surgery mid pretraining: SWA+CLA hybrid hurt SWE bench at equal perplexity → back to full attn, YOCO kept"

Hume AI introduced its Audio Decision API, which answers plain language questions about audio, such as how many speakers there are, whether anyone is laughing, or whether a caller is frustrated. Hume says it replaces what used to take 10+ separate models, was trained on more than 10M human judgments, and works in realtime or across hours of audio. Voice agents and call analytics have been stitching together many narrow audio models; a single queryable audio understanding endpoint lowers the bar to build them.

@hume_ai posted: "Introducing the Hume Audio Decision API. Ask anything: How many speakers? Is anyone laughing? Is the caller frustrated? What used to take 10+ models is now one call. Trained on 10M+ human judgments, so it answers the way human listeners would. Realtime, or across hours of"

@hume_ai added: "For those curious about batch mode: this uncut demo shows the Hume Audio Decision API working through hours of recorded calls, in real time. Reading this post probably took longer than the processing did. All your questions. All your audio. Confidence scores on every answer." @hume_ai added: "@andyhq Now we will know who should and shouldn't be handled properly"

&Tutor scored best on a widely used tutor model eval, the company says. A product launch betting on modeling humans, not just tasks.

@humansand posted: "Introducing Ampersand Tutor (&Tutor), we trained a model to learn like people, then trained &Tutor to teach it On a widely used tutor model eval, &Tutor performed best, showing how better models of people can help build AI for humans Available through our API, try it!"

@freeCodeCamp added: "Healthcare data is sensitive, so sending it to a cloud AI service isn't always an option. In this tutorial, Lakshmi shows you how to build a local healthcare AI assistant with MedGemma, Ollama, and Open WebUI. You'll run the model on your own machine, chat with a medically" @GithubProjects added: "Most RAG tutorials skip the hard parts, so build a modular Agentic RAG with LangGraph that adds conversation memory and query clarification. Explore it here:"

Cartesia's new platform co designs its voice models and agent harness, claiming #1 on Artificial Analysis Speech Agent Arena and ServiceNow EVA Bench. Voice agents are moving from model APIs to full stack platforms; Cartesia is going after enterprise contact centers directly.

@cartesia posted: "Introducing Cartesia Managed Agents: our end to end voice agent platform for enterprise. #1 on @ArtificialAnlys Speech Agent Arena and @ServiceNow EVA Bench, built by co designing our voice models and harness as one system. Check it out →"

@marctechireai added: "@cartesia launched Managed Agents, an end to end voice agent platform for enterprise. $0.03 a minute, 44 languages and outbound batches of up to 5,000 numbers."

Moonshot AI launched 3D Make in Kimi, which connects to tools like Blender and CAD software to create 3D models, assemble scenes and develop games. It is available now on Kimi and Kimi Work. A day after adding Google workspace connectors, Kimi is pushing its agent into hands on creative and engineering software, where tool using agents are starting to compete.

@Kimi_Moonshot posted: "Introducing 3D Make in Kimi. Kimi connects to tools like Blender and CAD tools to create models, assemble scenes, and develop games. Available now on and Kimi Work."

@AINativeF added: "⭐ Today’s China AI Native Industry Insights include: 1. Moonshot AI Launches 3D Make Feature in Kimi for Model and Game Creation 2. Meituan LongCat unveils LongCat 2.5, a 1.6T parameter multimodal agentic model 3. Tencent Unveils Hyra 2.0, Next Version of Hunyuan Research" @AINativeF added: "Moonshot AI Launches 3D Make Feature in Kimi for Model and Game Creation Moonshot AI introduced a new feature called 3D Make within its Kimi AI assistant. The feature connects Kimi to external tools such as Blender and CAD software to create 3D models, assemble scenes, and"

New Anthropic research tests whether Claude agents can automate subtle misalignment red teaming that is usually limited by researcher time. Safety testing that scales with compute instead of human hours changes how fast labs can vet models.

@AnthropicAI posted: "New Anthropic research: Automating subtle emergent misalignment research. Red teaming is a vital part of model safety testing, but it’s also limited by researchers’ time. To see if we could automate it, we set thousands of Claude agents the task of evading a training data"

@JamesMalsawm added: "@AndrewCurran_ hacked anthropic's slack, now red teams for openai.. awkward much?"

Coverage of Anthropic's S 1 highlights a reported $80B loss, while 2x Anthropic ETFs are filed but not yet live. First frontier lab IPO sets the public market read on AI lab economics.

@LifeboatHQ posted: "Anthropic Reports $80 Billion Loss in IPO Filing"

@apples_jimmy added: "So, what does this mean for the Anthropic IPO ? Dario get your data centres in space" @dillon_mulroy added: "@choblin29 @grok based on anthropics recent s1 filing how much revenue does each employee generate"

a16z's charts of the week show cheaper tokens are not lowering AI bills, and its partners describe a widening 'rack spread' as GPU prices rise while token prices fall. Rising GPU costs against falling token prices squeeze inference margins for AI labs and the clouds that serve them.

@a16z posted: "Cheaper tokens don't mean cheaper bills Since May:, Average token prices are down 67%, Token spend is up more than 120% Charts of the Week:"

@scaling01 added: "Despite Opus 5.5’s token use and spending setting weekly records, Anthropic’s overall weekly spend remains just below its July high because of falling token prices" @AgenticAIFdn added: "Cutting token spend can look like a win until a cheaper model drops quality or creates more work. Tokens are only part of what AI costs. J.R. Storment of the Tokenomics Foundation explains the Total Cost of AI, and why value matters as much as spend."

Open weights DeepSeek V4.1 Flash tops Papers with Code usage as researchers build on it; resellers bundle cheap Flash access. Open frontier class weights are becoming the default research base.

@NielsRogge posted: "Unsurprisingly, DeepSeek v4.1 Flash is the most popular method on @paperswithcodee ! When you open source one of the best LLMs, all research gets built on top of it :)"

@UnslothAI added: "We’re releasing new DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 GGUFs, ranking highest across benchmarks. Dynamic V3.1 leads across nearly all benchmarks: GPQA Diamond, AIME25, LiveCodeBench v6, MMLU Pro, Aider & more. Run on 64GB+ RAM. GGUF: Blog:" @RunhanH added: "Huge congrats to the OpenRUA team! Exciting to see open source LLMs powering robot control We’re also pushing in the same direction with RAX, using the fully open source DeepSeek V4.1 Flash as our base LLM with support for self hosted inference. RAX harness +"

3D OS gives coding agents 80 skills across animation, rigging, art and previs. Agents are being packaged for creative 3D pipelines.

@emmanuel_2m posted: "We just open sourced an entire 3D studio. Introducing 3D OS by Scenario. It gives your coding agent 6 departments and 80 skills. 3D OS ┃ ┣ Animation ┃ ┣ Rigging Any Character ┃ ┗ Motion From a Video ┃ ┣ Art ┃ ┣ Previs and Storyboards ┃ ┗"

@retellai added: "Retell ranked #1 overall in the @G2dotcom × @getbluejay_ai's Voice AI Agent Platform Evaluation. 6 platforms were tested on the same 90 simulated call scenarios, 5 times each, across healthcare, legal, and retail. Success meant completing the caller’s request and taking the" @physorg_com added: "U.S. building losses from fires could nearly triple by 2060 under a scenario with moderate climate action but no new fire protections. @NatureComms"

SpeRID Labs released Idol, a 600M parameter 3D diffusion transformer trained from scratch that runs in under 7.2GB of VRAM. Small, open 3D generators make asset creation cheap for games, AR and spatial apps.

@speridlabs posted: "Meet Idol, an open image to 3D model. A 3D DiT that generates complete 3D objects from just an image. Pre trained from scratch, contains only 600M parameters, it runs on consumer GPUs, using less than 7.2 GB of VRAM and can generate 1M splats in 3 seconds. Try it today. Fully"

@dextune added: "Idol Turbo The Idol image to 3D model now finishes in about 0.75 seconds on an H200 This is a faster version of Idol, the single image to 3D Gaussian model I shared earlier. It still takes one object photo and outputs up to a million 3D Gaussians. Idol needs 50 steps with"

A new tour of Neuralink shows the R1 implant robot up close; separate reports say Neuralink is testing iPhone calls via its speech implant. BCI is moving from lab demos toward consumer device integration.

