@PairedWith_P7 added: "Using only mind power to drive the wheelchair with my Neuralink N1 implant." @maticrobots added: "@DigitalAssetsUS @elonmusk @neuralink @DeadManRolln @JoshWest247 Welcome to the fam!"

Several builders posted that an official Hermes Agent mobile beta from Nous Research is out for iOS and Android, letting people reach their agent from their phone; some testers note access is still limited. Open agent frameworks are following commercial assistants onto phones, where people actually check in on agents.

@dedene posted: "The official Hermes Mobile beta for iOS and Android can be downloaded from here:"

@TechMDAI added: "Love that your able to have Double Hermes agents on IPhone Duo and with the new Hermes mobile app @Teknium" @HermesWatcher added: "@Teknium @ToniRMCardoso For the record, the "LEAK" was @NousResearch own website. The page literally says "You are invited to the Hermes Mobile Beta.""

Per The Information, Meta and Fourier built the ~106 lb robot, codenamed Max, in roughly three months; a second version started in September. Meta says the units are for internal research and not for sale. Meta pairing its AI agent work with a Chinese humanoid maker shows how fast big platforms are moving into robot bodies.

@AIRoboticsInt posted: "JUST IN: Meta built a 106 pound humanoid robot in just 3 months, with Chinese startup Fourier. $META • Codename: Max • A second version is already in the works • Meta says it has no plans to sell these units Per The Information."

@lukas_m_ziegler added: "What a week in robotics. I summarized the most important news from the physical AI space. → Tesla is building Optimus on a production line in Fremont. Outside customers get their first robots in 2028. → Meta built a humanoid called Max with Fourier in just 3 months. →" @theinformation added: "Meta built a humanoid robot prototype with China’s Fourier in just three months but the collaboration comes with geopolitical complications. The Information’s Jyoti Mann explains: “Neither government is necessarily thrilled about the broader implications of such a cooperation"

After the FCC exempted Lightberry's Lumi from its foreign humanoid sales block on Oct 30, builders are picking apart its $39,990 price plus $20,000/yr software subscription. First humanoid cleared under the FCC rule sets the template for US humanoid pricing and compliance.

@IlirAliu_ posted: "Lightberry wants to charge $20,000 a year for software running on a $39,990 humanoid robot. That's $40,000 for the robot and another $40,000 in software fees over the following two years. Breakdown #002: @lightberry The San Francisco startup, founded by @aliattar and @sadkoeni"

@roboticomarket added: "@lightberry's Lumi humanoid has received a conditional exemption from the FCC's Covered List following a U.S. national security review. The approval is tied to plans for U.S. manufacturing and continued product vetting."

Pika says H3 Flex tops DesignArena's open weight text to video board, with 4K output and audio stems. Open weight video models are closing on closed ones.

@Axylusion posted: ".@pika_labs launched Pika H3 Flex. They say it sits at number one among open weight text to video models on DesignArena's board, with exportable audio stems, up to 4K, and a 15 second clip with soundtrack in as little as 7 seconds. Weights are out from day one. Vendor"

@ComfyUI added: "A Halloween music video made with Pika's new H3 Flex model Now in ComfyUI with 6 step video generation, synchronized audio, and reference guided generation. To try this workflow, link below" @MireloAI added: "Not even the music can hide the poor audio timing and quality of @pika_labs' H3 Flex. It's fast, but like many video models, its audio lacks fidelity and sync. If the audio is off, the whole video feels off. Use Mirelo SFX 2.0 for sound that actually fits your video"

ComfyUI added Pika's H3 Flex model with 6 step video generation, synchronized audio, and reference guided generation, showing it off with a Halloween music video. Fast few step video models arriving in open node workflows put studio style generation in creators' local pipelines.

@ComfyUI posted: "A Halloween music video made with Pika's new H3 Flex model Now in ComfyUI with 6 step video generation, synchronized audio, and reference guided generation. To try this workflow, link below"

@wild_zones added: "@indystryfilms I still prefer the base overall for most things but the 1.1 lightx2v is indeed solid…was hands down my choice for speed until Pika Flex. Now mostly tossup depending on case…still testing a ton. FastH3 is mostly ok too but have found to be less consistently useable overall."

WIRED published an exclusive early look at Apple's touchscreen MacBook Pro, and 9to5Mac detailed how the new MacBook Pro and macOS 27 are optimized for touch. Apple long said it would never put touch on the Mac, so this changes how developers design Mac apps.

@WIRED posted: "An exclusive early look at Apple’s new touchscreen MacBook Pro the computer Apple said would never happen."

@cultofmac added: "Don’t worry, touchscreen MacBook Pro doesn’t mean iPad will go away dlvr.​it/TW98Hc"

Developers can set up ElevenLabs with one prompt to a coding agent, which runs the CLI and adds the API key. Dev tools are being redesigned for coding agents as the main user.

@ElevenLabsDevs posted: "Our onboarding is now agent first. You can set up ElevenLabs in your environment with one prompt to your coding agent. The agent runs the ElevenLabs CLI and adds an API key to your project. Your only manual step is clicking Authorize in the browser."

@smallest_AI added: "Pulse Pro is the #1 speech to text model for English accuracy. On Bluejay Labs' independent MISTT benchmark, it outperformed 82 other models on real voice agent calls and doctor patient consults, including Amazon, NVIDIA, ElevenLabs and Deepgram. See the full leaderboard in the" @ElevenLabsDevs added: "Our agent skills have passed 110k downloads. The 12 skills give your coding agent Text to Speech, Speech to Text, ElevenAgents, music, dubbing, and more. > npx skills add elevenlabs/skills"

AI influencers flag OpenAI's plan to bring Chat and Work together and warn that regular chat may draw from the same quota as Codex. Product consolidation at OpenAI could quietly cut effective limits for the heaviest users, the same people who set the mood around the product.

@hqmank posted: "OpenAI plans to bring Chat and Work together. I'm worried about the usage limits. I do a huge amount of work in ChatGPT's web app. If regular Chat has to share my Codex quota too, I'll need a much higher limit to keep doing the same work."

@EvanKirstel added: "OpenAI is testing a ChatGPT that merges the Chat and Work tabs into one place. Makes sense. People don't split their day into chatting and working, and a single workspace is where enterprise adoption gets sticky. @OpenAI" @geoffinosaka added: "@Henryf1w No, OpenAI are planning to boost Google search in December when they merge chat and work. A lot of folk with be driven back to google as they don't want to drain their new token pool or simple questions and searches."

Glif lets creators cast AI models and generate ad content end to end, working across video models like Seedance. AI creative agents are moving into commercial ad production.

@heyglif posted: "Today we’re launching the first AI modeling agency right inside Glif. Cast hundreds of real AI models, drop your product, idea or brief and let Glif create super compelling content for you end to end. If you still think AI models aren’t ready for real work, watch this"

@OpenMed_AI added: "Know a model's blind spot before you run it on your notes. GLiNER2.5 BioNER Disease Base's is bare abbreviations. On this problem list line it returns only "acute exacerbation of COPD" and misses COPD, CHF, T2DM and OSA. GLiFormer BioNER Disease Base finds all four." @OpenMed_AI added: "OpenMed 3.2 is out. Four new open disease models, one analyze_text call: GLiFormer Base and Large, GLiNER2.5 Base and Spanish. Apache 2.0, on CPU. Also new: Nepali IDs and UK postcodes in de identification, and synthetic FHIR, OMOP, HL7 and C CDA data."

Sternstein argues technical hiring is running hot and the AI jobs panic is not showing up in postings. A data counterpoint to the AI layoffs narrative from a major AI investor.

@a16z posted: "a16z's Moses Sternstein on the AI jobs panic, what job postings actually show, and why technical hiring is running hot: "People are saying, 'Oh, AI's already killing jobs.'... but right now it's almost certainly not." "Even human resources, right? Which is considered the front"

@a16z added: "a16z's Moses Sternstein and Alex Danco on the "rack spread," the gap between rising GPU prices and falling token prices: "The core observation in the rack spread is as follows: roughly since the summer, GPU pricing has gone up, token pricing has gone down. Why that is: a"

Xanadu's whitepaper with AMD describes Backline, which coordinates CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs and ASICs to hit the microsecond feedback loops needed for real time quantum error correction. Real time error correction is a gating problem for useful quantum machines, and it pulls classical chipmakers into the stack.

@XanaduAI posted: "Achieving real time, heterogeneous quantum classical co processing is incredibly hard, but for the end user, it no longer has to be complicated. In our recent whitepaper, we worked with @AMD to explain how we are solving the co processing bottleneck with Backline:"

@XanaduAI added: "Co designed with @AMD, Backline is an open platform for low latency execution that dynamically connects quantum workloads to the right classical engine, CPU, GPU, FPGA. Built into @PennyLaneAI. Open source. Available today. #QuantumComputing"

Posts report two US attacks on Iranian military sites around Hormuz within hours. Energy markets and shipping lanes are exposed; watch oil and defense stocks.

